{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310798,"nonce":"4oxL4KmdtdlyRxHJFk0+wykXcNI=","title":"LLVM JIT discarded every inline intrinsic wrapper at the call site; fixing it wins on array ops but Math.sqrt as llvm.sqrt.f64 REGRESSES ...","message":"`llvm_jit_backend.rs` builds `alwaysinline` wrappers for externs that have a\none-instruction form (`haxe_math_sqrt`→`llvm.sqrt.f64`, `haxe_array_length`→a\nload, `Std.int`→fptosi). The wrapper carries the runtime symbol's NAME, and the\ncall site resolved that name to the runtime address and emitted\n`call inttoptr (i64 \u003caddr\u003e to ptr)` — so **every wrapper was silently discarded\nand the whole intrinsic path was dead code**. Confirmed in\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR=1` → `/tmp/rayzor_llvm_ir_opt.ll`.\n\nFIXED e0b8c63c with a structural guard, not a name list: a function we emitted a\nbody for is ours (`func.count_basic_blocks() \u003e 0`); only a bodyless declaration\nmay resolve to a runtime address. nbody 0.73 -\u003e 0.68s (array length was **26% of\nnbody**, ~20M calls, visible as a leaf in `sample`).\n\n\nLetting the math wrapper through makes nbody **2.4x SLOWER** (0.68 -\u003e 1.65s),\nreproducible, interleaved, warm. Kept out via `math_intrinsic_ids`.\n\n- The emitted assembly is strictly BETTER: 106 vs 153 instructions in `advance`,\n  `fsqrt` replacing a `blr`. Reading assembly would tell you to ship it.\n- Mechanism: with the call gone, LLVM pairs `a.z`+`a.vx` into one `ldp`; the\n  next iteration's pair straddles `a.vx`, written by the previous iteration, so\n  **store-to-load forwarding fails every iteration**. The call blocks the pairing.\n- NOT reproducible in a micro-benchmark: a bare `Math.sqrt` loop and a\n  sqrt-feeding-a-divide loop are both identical either way (0.19s). It needs the\n  loop that also STORES to the object it reads.\n- Fast-math flags on the intrinsic call do NOT help (1.64 vs 1.66).\n- Ruled out: Cranelift fallback (Cranelift-only is 0.68s), compile time (equal,\n  ~0.08s once warm), inlining of `advance` into main (happens in BOTH).\n\n\n- A cold run after a compiler rebuild costs ~0.35s (BLADE keyed on compiler\n  hash). My first A/B compared a WARM baseline against a COLD candidate and\n  produced a bogus \"compile time went 0.068 -\u003e 0.44s\". Warm every binary, then\n  interleave. See [[feedback_perf_measurement_protocol]].\n- Separate compile from execute by running the SAME kernel at two workloads\n  (nbody at 500k and 10M advances) and solving T = C + n·E.\n- `llc -mcpu=apple-m1` on the dumped IR is not what MCJIT runs; use it to read\n  codegen, never to conclude a win.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_llvm_jit_intrinsic_wrappers_and_sqrt_trap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}